<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tortola</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tortola is the largest and most populous of the British Virgin Islands, with the capital, Road Town, on its south coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers held it before English forces seized it in 1672, and it became the centre of a small colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantations worked by enslaved Africans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tortola.jpg" alt="Tortola" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Henry A-W (Henry aw 23:34, 10 July 2006 (UTC)) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roadtown,_Tortola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tortola is a mountainous island of volcanic origin, 19 kilometres long and 5 kilometres wide, with an area of 55.7 square kilometres. Its highest peak is Mount Sage, at 530 metres, and it lies near an earthquake fault, so minor earthquakes are common. Road Town, on its south coast, is the capital of the British Virgin Islands. The name may be an anglicisation of the island's original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> name Ter-Tholen, after the Dutch island of Tholen; some sources say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> named it Tortola, Spanish for turtle dove, while others say he called it Santa Ana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On his second voyage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> sighted the Virgin Islands and named the archipelago after the 11,000 virgins of the martyr Saint Ursula. The Spanish made a few attempts to settle there, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blackbeard">Blackbeard</a> and Captain Kidd were the first permanent residents.</p><p>The English then established a plantation colony on Tortola and the surrounding islands, and large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> plantations depended on the labour of enslaved Africans. Most early settlers came in the late 18th century, when Loyalists from the Thirteen Colonies received land grants from the Crown after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, bringing enslaved people who outnumbered the colonists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> dominated the island's economy for more than a century.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> abolished the international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> settled Africans freed from illegal slave ships at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingstown">Kingstown</a> on Tortola, where St Phillip's Church was built as one of the earliest free black churches in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in the British colonies in 1834, planters found it hard to make a profit from free labour, and competition from beet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> added to the downturn. Many white landowners left, and an earthquake and tsunami struck in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tortola is the largest and most populous of the British Virgin Islands, which are under the British flag but use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> dollar as their official currency because of their trade with the neighbouring American islands. Financial services are a major part of the economy, and the International Business Companies Act of the early 1980s encouraged offshore companies.</p><p>In the late 1970s the activist Noel Lloyd led a protest that stopped a plan to lease a large part of the neighbouring island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anegada">Anegada</a> for 199 years, and a park on Tortola is named for him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-irma">Hurricane Irma</a> caused extensive damage across the islands in September 2017, most severely on Tortola.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tortola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1997 the Dominica Carib artist Jacob Frederick and the <b>Tortola</b> artist Aragorn Dick Read built a traditional canoe on the model of the fishing canoes still used in Dominica, Guadeloupe and Martinique, and took it on a voyage to the Orinoco delta to meet the Kalinago communities…</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch buccaneers and settlers had a foothold on <b>Tortola</b> when English forces from the Leeward Islands seized it in 1672.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-virgin-islands">British Virgin Islands</a></li></ul></section>
